memory_encode_2026-09-18.tsv — peak heap of one `zenjpegai encode` process, heaptrack 1.5
(peak heap memory consumption; peak_rss includes heaptrack's own overhead). Produced by
scripts/bench/memory_encode.sh (E8, encoder limits and memory).

host:    AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D, 32 threads, Linux 7.0, rustc 1.98.1, release profile,
         default features + cli
images:  <ref>/data/test/00030_TE_560x888_8bit_sRGB.png (img30_560x888) and
         <ref>/data/test/00001_TE_2096x1400_8bit_sRGB.png (img01_2096x1400) —
         the reference software's own test images
modes:   encode = fixed model 1, beta-disp 0, BOP (CLI: --model 1 --beta-disp 0 --op bop)
         bpp050 = rate matched to 0.5 bpp (CLI: --bpp 0.5 --op bop; the search runs all
         four models' analysis once, keeps the best, then tries beta displacements)
threads: 32 = rayon pool, 1 = --single-thread

labels
  encode        default recycled-buffer pool (1 GiB): pooled buffers stay resident,
                so peak heap exceeds the live bytes the encode itself needs
  encode-pool0  --pool-mb 0: no buffer recycling = the memory the encode itself holds;
                the EncodeLimits / estimate_encode_memory model is calibrated on these rows

estimate_encode_memory (BOP) versus the measured pool0 peak heap:
  560x888   fixed:         357.3 MB est / 266.5 MB measured = 1.34x
  560x888   rate matched:  447.3 MB est / 344.9 MB measured = 1.30x
  2096x1400 fixed:         857.5 MB est / 593.8 MB measured = 1.44x
  2096x1400 rate matched:  957.3 MB est / 682.1 MB measured = 1.40x
The E8 gate is "estimate within 1x-2x of measured"; tests/api_ref.rs pins these ratios.
